{"id":"80ed78a8-8640-47da-816a-e3aeec2e3b06","object":"chat.completion","created":1793329765,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"All three mechanisms are exposed (EnvVar descriptor + rearm functions, NewLineFormatter + _setup_logger, and the queue consumer `_log_worker`). Applying the environ.py edit first.\n\n```python\nr = replace_text(\n    \"deepinfer/environ.py\",\n    '''def _str(v: str | None, default: str = \"\") -> str:\n    return v if v is not None else default\n''',\n    '''def _str(v: str | None, default: str = \"\") -> str:\n    return v if v is not None else default\n\n\ndef _int_or_none_min(v: str | None, minimum: int = 32) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"Parse an optional int; unset means no limit, otherwise must be >= minimum.\"\"\"\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return value\n''',\n    'fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218',\n)\nprint(r)\n```","reasoning_content":"I have all needed mechanisms exposed. Now make edits.\n\n1. environ.py: add a factory method (optional) or just a lambda descriptor. Requirement: attribute value None when unset; parse int, must be >=32; ValueError message includes variable name (descriptor wraps with name already). Add to Logging section:\n\nDEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(lambda v: None if v is None else _int(v))  — but need >=32 check. Simpler: add a factory method `int_or_none_min(*, minimum=32)`? Requirement says \"可增加必要标准库辅助函数... 描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\". Let's add a helper function in environ module or a factory. I'll add a factory `EnvVar.positive_int_or_none(*, minimum)`? Keep it simple with a module-level helper:\n\ndef _int_or_none_min(v, minimum=32) -> int | None:\n    if v is None: return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return value\n\nNote empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError \"invalid literal for int()...\" with v='' — error wrapped with name. Good, and empty string should raise ValueError — yes int(\"\") raises ValueError. But note requirement: 空串抛ValueError. Good.\n\nWait: does unset variable yield None? os.getenv returns None. But empty string set to \"\" → int(\"\") ValueError. Good.\n\n2. logger.py: implement formatter budget.\n\nCompute: msg = super().format(record) gives formatted text including prefix. We need to limit record.message, which is computed by super().format via record.getMessage() (sets record.message). Actually logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(). So after super().format(record), we can use record.message. But we must not modify record.message... Actually record.message is set by formatter; modifying is risky—the requirement says not to modify msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. record.message is a formatted attribute; better compute ourselves? Safer: replace the occurrence of record.message in msg with truncated version, only if len > budget. But record.message may not appear identically? It does appear as the %(message)s substring. Approach: compute message text from record.getMessage() separately:\n\nfull_message = record.getMessage()\nif keep_full (record.keep_full_message is True) → no truncate.\nelif self.max_message_chars is not None and len(full_message) > budget: build truncated, then replace in msg.\n\nBut super().format already called getMessage and set record.message; calling record.getMessage() again could re-apply args — fine but if args is a dict etc. It's idempotent. However, to avoid double work, use record.message after super().format(record): record.message is the message text. But careful: super().format(record) also caches record.exc_text. Using record.message is fine, no mutation.\n\nBut note requirement 5: 沿用原有多行前缀... The original format implementation uses record.message for multi-line alignment. We need truncation before? Order: formats message; then truncates message (before multi-line alignment? Actually if truncated text contains newline... The truncated message replaces middle, so newlines could still exist in head/tail). Best: compute message truncation first, then produce final formatted string? But super().format builds full string with prefix and exception text. The truncation must be applied to message within msg. Approach:\n\nmsg = super().format(record)  # sets record.message\n...\nmessage = record.message\ntruncated = self._truncate(message)\nif truncated is not message:\n    msg = msg.replace(message, truncated, 1)  # careful: prefix could contain message accidentally? prefix is like \"[INFO 09-30 ...]\", message unlikely to appear there. But safer use the known structure: msg = record.message generally is the %(message)s position. Use msg.replace(message, truncated) but only once—actually original code used msg.find(record.message). Use index-based:\n\n    idx = msg.find(message)\n    msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx+len(message):]\n\nHmm but exception text appended after message—fine, preserved.\n\nAlternative cleaner: format the message before calling super, then temporarily... no, don't mutate record.\n\nWait, edge case: `%` in message with args? msg contains message literally once. Good.\n\nThen multi-line alignment uses record.message for prefix computation — original: `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]`. If msg no longer contains record.message (truncated), find returns -1 → prefix becomes msg[:-1] weird. So must handle: use truncated message for prefix detection. Compute inside: after truncation, do the alignment using the truncated text. Let's restructure:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        message = record.message\n        truncated = self._truncate_message(message, record)\n        if truncated is not message:\n            start = msg.find(message)\n            if start >= 0:\n                msg = msg[:start] + truncated + msg[start + len(message):]\n                message = truncated\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            start = msg.find(message)\n            if start >= 0:\n                prefix = msg[:start]\n                msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm careful: replacing newlines with prefix when prefix contains newlines... original code same. Keep close to original but guard with find>=0. Actually original had `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` — if record.message empty, guarded. Keep original logic but with variable.\n\nBut wait: colors — newline replacement adds prefix inside ANSI? Original applies color after. Keep same order.\n\nTruncation function:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n@staticmethod\ndef _truncate(message, record):\n    budget = max_chars\n    if budget is None or len(message) <= budget: return message\n    if getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True: return message\n    keep = budget - len(_OMITTED)\n    head = (keep + 1) // 2\n    tail = keep - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED + message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else message[:head] + _OMITTED\n```\n\nCareful when keep<=0 — budget>=32, marker len 15, keep>=17. But marker length: \"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15. Yes.\n\nCheck example budget 32: keep=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\nRequirement: keep_full_message check only when truncation needed? Order: check keep_full first maybe; either fine. But must be `is True`.\n\nNote: `record.message` attribute set by super().format; getattr fine.\n\nAlso max_message_chars param validation in __init__: keyword-only, None or int>=32, bool excluded.\n\nAlso default init: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Import environ in logger — check for circular imports: environ imports nothing from logger. logger currently doesn't import environ. environment imports os, threading etc. Fine to `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. But is logger imported by environ? No. Let's check whether other modules import logger before environ... fine.\n\nBut careful: init_logger sets up handler at import time? _setup_logger at module bottom likely. Let's check logger lines 69-107 and beyond — we have 75-90 and 96-107. There's likely `_setup_logger()` at end? Also multiprocessing patch stuff. Reading environ value at _setup_logger time: envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS may raise ValueError if invalid — that's acceptable per tests? Test likely sets valid values. But the module-level _setup_logger() call at import time means invalid env raises at import. Hmm, required? \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". Fine.\n\nBetter: import the module lazily inside _setup_logger to avoid circular import risk: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Use that.\n\n3. stats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`. Only one line.\n\nLet me test compute the truncation. Also need budget where message length > budget.\n\nNow write edits.\n\nLet me do environ edit first."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9734,"completion_tokens":2096,"total_tokens":11830,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1841},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5638},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}